# Fork-owned files and fork edits inside upstream files.
#
# This list is the secondary guardrail behind the conflict abort in
# sync-upstream.yml. On any merge conflict the sync aborts and opens a
# `sync`-labeled issue. But a *clean* merge can still silently overwrite fork
# code when upstream is not touching the same lines — the merge succeeds, the
# fork's change vanishes, and nobody notices until a provider stops working.
#
# So: after every clean merge, sync-upstream.yml verifies that each file below
# still exists on `sync` and still contains its survival marker. If a marker is
# gone, the sync fails, opens an issue, and refuses to push.
#
# Each entry: <path>  ::  <survival-marker-string>
#
# When you add or change a fork-owned file:
#   1. Add the file here with a marker UNIQUE TO THE FORK — an exported
#      identifier, config field, or literal that upstream does not contain.
#      Verify it with:
#          grep -c -F '<marker>' <path>                      # must be > 0
#          git show upstream/main:<path> | grep -c -F '<marker>'   # must be 0
#   2. Re-run the sync workflow (`workflow_dispatch`) to confirm it still passes.
#
# Do not remove entries unless the fork feature was intentionally retired AND
# the upstream merge removed the source code.

